---
import editorShot from "../assets/screens/editor.webp";
import lightShot from "../assets/screens/hero-light.webp";
import paletteShot from "../assets/screens/palette.webp";
import permissionsShot from "../assets/screens/permissions.webp";
import connectionShot from "../assets/screens/new-connection.webp";
import { REPO_URL } from "../lib/releases";
---

<section class="section" id="features">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="section-head">
      <p class="eyebrow">What’s inside</p>
      <h2>Everything for moving files. Nothing to fight.</h2>
      <p class="lead">
        Every pane, dialog and shortcut serves one loop: pick files, move them, see
        exactly what happened.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="bento">
      <article class="tile tile-wide tile-white">
        <div class="tile-copy">
          <p class="eyebrow">Protocols</p>
          <h3>FTP, FTPS and SFTP, without extra moving parts</h3>
          <p>
            Native Rust networking for all of them. TLS with certificate pins, strict
            SSH host keys and passive, extended passive or active data channels.
          </p>
          <p class="tile-facts">
            <span>Keychain credentials</span>
            <span>Host key verification</span>
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="tile-media" style={`--media-ratio: ${connectionShot.width} / ${connectionShot.height}`}>
          <img
            src={connectionShot.src}
            width={connectionShot.width}
            height={connectionShot.height}
            alt="New connection dialog with name, protocol, host, authentication and data channel fields"
            loading="lazy"
            decoding="async"
          />
        </div>
      </article>

      <article class="tile tile-narrow tile-peach">
        <div class="tile-copy">
          <p class="eyebrow">Editor</p>
          <h3>Edit remote files in place</h3>
          <p>
            Open a UTF-8 file up to 2&nbsp;MiB, edit it and save atomically. If it changed
            on the server since you opened it, sftply asks instead of overwriting
            someone else’s work.
          </p>
          <p class="tile-facts">
            <span>No autosave</span>
            <span>Stale-write detection</span>
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="tile-media" style={`--media-ratio: ${editorShot.width} / ${editorShot.height}`}>
          <img
            src={editorShot.src}
            width={editorShot.width}
            height={editorShot.height}
            alt="Editor with design-notes.md open on the remote server"
            loading="lazy"
            decoding="async"
          />
        </div>
      </article>

      <article class="tile tile-narrow tile-lavender">
        <div class="tile-copy">
          <p class="eyebrow">Keyboard</p>
          <h3>Built to be driven without the mouse</h3>
          <p>
            <code translate="no">⌘K</code> opens the command palette, <code translate="no">⌘1</code> and{" "}
            <code translate="no">⌘2</code> jump between panes and <code translate="no">⌘J</code> toggles the queue.
            Every action has a shortcut, and the hints follow your platform.
          </p>
          <p class="tile-facts">
            <span>Command palette</span>
            <span>Type-ahead</span>
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="tile-media" style={`--media-ratio: ${paletteShot.width} / ${paletteShot.height}`}>
          <img
            src={paletteShot.src}
            width={paletteShot.width}
            height={paletteShot.height}
            alt="Command palette searching for connections and actions"
            loading="lazy"
            decoding="async"
          />
        </div>
      </article>

      <article class="tile tile-wide tile-mint">
        <div class="tile-copy">
          <p class="eyebrow">Themes</p>
          <h3>Dark by default. Light as a first-class mode.</h3>
          <p>
            Named palettes, contrast-tested text and visible focus rings in every
            theme. The interface stays quiet so the files are the loudest thing on
            screen.
          </p>
          <p class="tile-facts">
            <span>System theme</span>
            <span>Contrast checked</span>
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="tile-media" style={`--media-ratio: ${lightShot.width} / ${lightShot.height}`}>
          <img
            src={lightShot.src}
            width={lightShot.width}
            height={lightShot.height}
            alt="Both panes and the transfer queue in the light theme"
            loading="lazy"
            decoding="async"
          />
        </div>
      </article>

      <article class="tile tile-wide tile-pink">
        <div class="tile-copy">
          <p class="eyebrow">Permissions</p>
          <h3>Unix modes, visible and editable</h3>
          <p>
            Octal and symbolic editing for a selection, with per-entry errors. When a
            server or platform cannot change modes, the action says so instead of
            failing halfway.
          </p>
          <p class="tile-facts">
            <span>Octal and symbolic</span>
            <span>Capability gated</span>
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="tile-media" style={`--media-ratio: ${permissionsShot.width} / ${permissionsShot.height}`}>
          <img
            src={permissionsShot.src}
            width={permissionsShot.width}
            height={permissionsShot.height}
            alt="Permissions dialog with an octal mode and read, write and execute checkboxes"
            loading="lazy"
            decoding="async"
          />
        </div>
      </article>

      <article class="tile tile-narrow tile-navy">
        <div class="tile-copy">
          <p class="eyebrow">Honest by construction</p>
          <h3>A queue that tells the truth</h3>
          <p>
            Most clients guess, then draw a progress bar and hope. sftply reports what
            the protocol actually did, including when the answer is “not supported”.
          </p>
        </div>

        <blockquote class="navy-quote">
          A transfer queue is a promise. If the server cannot resume a download, the
          interface should not offer a resume button.
        </blockquote>

        <ul class="navy-points">
          <li>
            <strong>Pause and resume only where the server allows it</strong>
            <span>If a transfer has to restart, the queue says so.</span>
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Cancelled is cancelled</strong>
            <span>A cancelled transfer never turns into a green row.</span>
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Failures are reported as failures</strong>
            <span>Errors carry a code, a safe message and a recovery action.</span>
          </li>
        </ul>

        <a class="tile-link" href={`${REPO_URL}/blob/main/docs/ARCHITECTURE.md`}>
          Read the architecture notes
        </a>
      </article>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
